"Internal Error ()\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/AbdelStark/tessera/issues/470\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/AbdelStark/tessera/issues/470: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGetting Started with CUDA Graphs | NVIDIA Technical Blog (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/cuda-graphs/)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/cuda-graphs/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 150\nL0: 【0†DEVELOPER】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Technical Blog】\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search blog] [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 【2†AR / VR】\nL7: \nL8: # Getting Started with CUDA Graphs\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Image†developer-blogs.nvidia.com】\nL11: \nL12: Sep 05, 2019\nL13: \nL14: By 【4†Alan Gray】\nL15: \nL16: [Button: Like ]\nL17: \nL18: 【5†Discuss (11)】\nL19: ## AI-Generated Summary\nL20:   * 【6†CUDA 10.1】 introduces CUDA Graphs to reduce GPU launch overhead by capturing multiple operations into a single executable graph.\nL21:   * In a test on an NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPU, a short kernel taking 2.9µs to execute incurred 9.6µs per launch with per-kernel synchronization, improved to 3.8µs by overlapping launches, and further reduced to 3.4µs using a captured and instantiated CUDA Graph.\nL22:   * Graph capture with cudaStreamBeginCapture and cudaStreamEndCapture, followed by cudaGraphInstantiate and repeated cudaGraphLaunch calls, amortizes one-time setup cost over many iterations.\nL23:   * The 【7†simpleCUDAGraphs†docs.nvidia.com】 sample demonstrates both stream capture and explicit API graph construction across multiple streams and GPUs.\nL24: ### Next Steps\nL25: \nL26:   * Read the 【8†CUDA Graphs section of the Programming Guide†docs.nvidia.com】 for detailed API reference and best practices.\nL27:   * Watch the 【9†GTC 2019 talk CUDA: New Features and Beyond†on-demand-gtc.gputechconf.com】 for a deeper technical overview.\nL28: \nL29: Powered by NVIDIA Nemotron. AI-generated content may summarize information incompletely. Verify important information. 【10†Learn more†www.nvidia.com】\nL30: The performance of GPU architectures continue to increase with every new generation. Modern GPUs are so fast that, in many cases of interest, the time taken by each GPU operation (e.g. kernel or memory copy) is now measured in microseconds. However, there are overheads associated with the submission of each operation to the GPU – also at the microsecond scale – which are now becoming significant in an increasing number of cases.\nL31: Real applications perform large numbers of GPU operations: a typical pattern involves many iterations (or timesteps), with multiple operations within each step. For example, simulations of molecular systems iterate over many timesteps, where the position of each molecule is updated at each step based on the forces exerted on it by the other molecules.\nL32: For a simulation technique to accurately model nature, typically multiple algorithmic stages corresponding to multiple GPU operations are required per timestep. If each of these operations is launched to the GPU separately, and completes quickly, then overheads can combine to form a significant overall degradation to performance.\nL33: 【11†CUDA Graphs】 have been designed to allow work to be defined as graphs rather than single operations. They address the above issue by providing a mechanism to launch multiple GPU operations through a single CPU operation, and hence reduce overheads. In this article, we demonstrate how to get started using CUDA Graphs, by showing how to augment a very simple example.\nL34: # The Example\nL35: \nL36: Consider a case where we have a sequence of short GPU kernels within each timestep:\nL37: \nL38:     Loop over timesteps\nL39:         …\nL40:         shortKernel1\nL41:         shortKernel2\nL42:         …\nL43:         shortKernelN\nL44:         …\n------------------