4: \nL65: Version: 4.0.0\nL66: \nL67: [Button: On this page]\nL68: # Sumcheck API Documentation\nL69: ## Overview【55†​】\nL70: \nL71: The Sumcheck protocol allows a Prover to prove to a Verifier that the sum of a multilinear polynomial, over the Boolean hypercube, equals a specific scalar value.\nL72: \nL73: For a polynomial $P$P with $n$n variables, the Prover aims to prove that:\nL74: \nL75: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} P(X_1, X_2,..., X_n) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​P(X 1​,X 2​,...,X n​)=C,\nL76: \nL77: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL78: The proof is constructed interactively, involving a series of $n$n rounds. Each round consists of a challenge (from the Verifier), a computation (from the Prover), and a verification step (by the Verifier). Using a Fiat-Shamir (FS) scheme, the proof becomes non-interactive, enabling the Prover to generate the entire proof and send it to the Verifier for validation.\nL79: ### Sumcheck with Combine Function【56†​】\nL80: \nL81: The Sumcheck protocol can be generalized to handle multiple multilinear polynomials. In this case, assuming there are $m$m polynomials of $n$n variables, and $f$f is an arbitrary function that takes these $m$m polynomials and returns a polynomial of equal or higher degree, the Prover tries to prove that:\nL82: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} f\\left(P_1(X_1, ..., X_n), P_2(X_1, ..., X_n), ..., P_m(X_1, ..., X_n)\\right) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​f(P 1​(X 1​,...,X n​),P 2​(X 1​,...,X n​),...,P m​(X 1​,...,X n​))=C,\nL83: \nL84: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL85: ## ICICLE's Sumecheck Implementation【57†​】\nL86: \nL87: ICICLE implements a non-interactive Sumcheck protocol that supports a combine function. It is available on both the CPU and CUDA backends of ICICLE. The polynomials are passed to the protocol in MLE (evaluation representation) form.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSET: Stream-Event-Triggered Scheduling for Efficient CUDA Graph Pipelines (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.05495v1)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2606.05495v1\",\"pattern\":\"github\"}); Total lines: 365\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Back to arXiv】\nL13: \nL14: 【1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 【2†Report Issue】 【3†Back to Abstract】 【4†Download PDF】\nL15:   1. 【5†Abstract】\nL16:   2. 【6†1 Introduction】\nL17:   3. 【7†2 Related Work】\nL18:   4. 【8†3 Background and Motivation】\nL19:     1. 【9†3.1 CUDA Graph】\nL20:     2. 【10†3.2 Bottleneck Analysis】\nL21:   5. 【11†4 Stream-Event-Triggered Scheduling】\nL22:     1. 【12†4.1 Job-as-Graph and Memory Management】\nL23:     2. 【13†4.2 Event-Chained Scheduling】\nL24:       1. 【14†Scheduler Components:】\nL25:       2. 【15†Initialization:】\nL26:       3. 【16†Job Submission】\nL27:       4. 【17†Job Dispatch and Launch】\nL28:       5. 【18†Asynchronous Resource Return】\nL29:   6. 【19†5 Experimental Evaluations】\nL30:     1. 【20†5.1 Evaluation Setup】\nL31:       1. 【21†Hardware Configuration】\nL32:       2. 【22†Benchmark Applications】\nL33:       3. 【23†Baseline Models】\nL34:     2. 【24†5.2 Performance (Throughput) Evaluation】\nL35:     3. 【25†5.3 Scheduling Overhead Analysis】\nL36:   7. 【26†6 Conclusions】\nL37:     1. 【27†Disclosure of Interests】\nL38:   8. 【28†References】\nL39: 【29†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org】\nL40: \nL41: arXiv:2606.05495v1 [cs.DC] 03 Jun 2026\nL42: # SET: Stream-Event-Triggered Scheduling for Efficient CUDA Graph Pipelines\nL43: \nL44: Zhengxiong Li✉ Affiliation: University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison WI, 53706, USA E-mail {zhengxiong.li, tsung-wei.huang, uogras}@wisc.edu    Tsung-Wei Huang Affiliation: University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison WI, 53706, USA E-mail {zhengxiong.li, tsung-wei.huang, uogras}@wisc.edu    Umit Ogras Affiliation: University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison WI, 53706, USA E-mail {zhengxiong.li, tsung-wei.huang, uogras}@wisc.edu\nL45: ###### Abstract\nL46: Achieving peak GPU performance remains a significant challenge as the system throughput is constrained by host-device synchronization delays and kernel scheduling overheads, even with aggressive kernel optimizations and batch processing. Furthermore, existing approaches often underutilize hardware resources such as compute cores and copy engines due to scheduling overheads.\nL47: To address these problems, we propose a CUDA runtime framework for task-parallel pipelines to minimize the synchronization overheads and the gap between kernel executions.\nL48: The proposed solution combines two innovations: (1) a multi-stream task-parallel pipeline programming model that leverages event-chaining and work-stealing mechanisms to fully utilize available hardware resources; (2) a graph-based execution flow with per-stream buffers to ensure memory safety for multiple in-flight jobs running concurrently.\nL49: Extensive evaluations on representative real-world workloads show 1.15–1.44$\\times$ speedup and reduce scheduling overheads by 18–54% compared to state-of-the-art CUDA graph baselines.\nL50: ###### Keywords:\nL51: \nL52: CUDA Graphs GPU Runtime Dynamic Task Scheduling.\nL53: ## 1 Introduction\nL54: Modern GPU applications, ranging from AI and machine learning to scientific and image processing workloads, execute as pipelines of interdependent kernels connected by data transfers and synchronization operations. To reduce the repeated cost of launching these kernels, NVIDIA introduced the CUDA Graph execution model, which captures a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of kernel launches and memory operations that can be efficiently instantiated and replayed [【30†4】].\nL349:   * [17] J. Zhong and B. He (2014) Kernelet: high-throughput gpu kernel executions with dynamic slicing and scheduling. IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems 25 (6), pp. 1522–1532. External Links: ISSN 1045-9219, 【95†Document†dx.doi.org】 Cited by: 【89†§2】.\nL350: Experimental support, please 【96†view the build logs】 for errors. Generated by 【97†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov】 .\nL351: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL352: \nL353: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL354: \nL355:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL356: \nL357: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL358: Our team has already identified 【98†the following issues†github.com】. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL359: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a 【99†list of packages that need conversion†github.com】, and welcome 【100†developer contributions†github.com】.\nL360: \nL361: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【101†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL362: 【102†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【103†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【104†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【105†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【106†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【107†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【108†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【109†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL363: \nL364: Major funding su